00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.880
 to force the airways back open.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:25.520
 And the classic example given is albuterol.

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:25.840
 Yes.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.160
 Albuterol is the prototypical rescue inhaler.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.880
 Millions of people carry it in their pockets every day.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.840
 And the real beauty of albuterol is its selectivity.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.680
 It knows what it's looking for.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:36.240
 Exactly.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.280
 It's chemically formulated to hunt down beta two receptors

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:42.240
 and mostly ignore the beta one and alpha receptors.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.920
 So when an asthmatic takes a puff, the drug goes straight into the lungs,

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.240
 hits the smooth muscle and physically forces the airways to relax and open.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:53.520
 But interestingly, if we look at a completely different respiratory issue,

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:59.760
 like a nasal congestion, the pharmacological approach is completely inverted.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:00.320
 Oh, yeah.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:01.360
 This is a great point.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.280
 The sources note that for a stuffy nose, you don't use a beta two drug.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.480
 Use a topical nasal spray that targets alpha one receptors.

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:12.880
 Drugs like oxymetazoline or phenylephrine.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:17.680
 And this really caught my eye because it explains the physical mechanism of congestion

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:20.640
 in a way most people I think completely misunderstand.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:24.080
 Oh, this is probably one of the greatest misconceptions in everyday health.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.760
 When you have a severe head cold and your nose is completely blocked,

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:31.920
 most people just assume their nasal passages are packed solid with thick mucus.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:32.960
 Yeah, that's what it feels like.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:33.440
 Right.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:38.320
 And while there is mucus, the primary reason you can't breathe is actually structural.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:41.360
 The tiny blood vessels lining the inside of your nose have become

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:45.280
 incredibly swollen and engorged with blood due to inflammation.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.840
 The tissue literally just swells shut.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:48.320
 Right.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:52.960
 So blowing your nose 100 times doesn't fix it because you're trying to blow out inflamed tissue.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:53.600
 Exactly.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:55.440
 It's like trying to blow out a swollen lip.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:57.280
 So how do we fix it?

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:01.040
 We use an alpha one agonist like oxymetazoline.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.120
 You spray it locally into the nose.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.680
 It hits those engorged blood vessels and remembers the alpha command, which is...

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:08.320
 Squeeze.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:09.200
 Squeeze.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.200
 The drug forces the blood vessels to violently constrict, clamping shut.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:17.440
 As the blood is squeezed out of the area, the swollen tissue rapidly shrinks back down.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:20.320
 And suddenly, the physical airway is open again.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.120
 That is just so deeply satisfying to understand.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:24.560
 Like finally, it makes sense.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.080
 Okay.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.800
 Now let's look at the cardiac indications.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:31.360
 Because this is where the stakes get incredibly high.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:36.720
 The notes call these drugs vasoactive adrenergics or pressers and inotropes.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:37.840
 Yes, pressers.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.200
 Very common in the ICU.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:39.360
 Right.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.840
 They're using the ICU to support a failing heart or treat profound shock.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:48.960
 We are given specific profiles for a few heavy hitters in the material,

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.760
 starting with probably the most famous one, epinephrine.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:52.640
 Epinephrine.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:57.520
 This is the frontline absolute ultimate rescue drug and emergency medicine.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:00.880
 The source defines it as an endogenous vasoactive

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:08.240
 catecholamine, but points out it is the prototypical non-selective adrenic agonist.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:11.920
 It's administered in massive emergencies, like advanced cardiac life support,

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:13.760
 when a patient's heart has completely stopped.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:18.080
 The key word in that entire definition is non-selective.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.000
 Because it hits everything.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:20.960
 Exactly.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.360
 Epinephrine does not care what receptor it hits.

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:25.040
 It does not pick and choose.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:27.200
 When you push epinephrine into a patient's vein,

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:31.920
 it floods the entire system, violently slamming into every alpha one,

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.400
 alpha two, beta one and beta two receptor.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:35.840
 It can possibly find.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:36.240
 Okay.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.920
 And this leads me to a major question.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:41.760
 If a drug is non-selective and hits literally everything,

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:45.840
 heart, lungs, blood vessels, digestion, pupils, all at once,

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.880
 isn't that the pharmacological equivalent of throwing a grenade into a room when

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:52.560
 you might just need a sniper rifle?

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:53.680
 A grenade, yeah.

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.200
 I mean, if a patient is crashing,

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:59.200
 why would you want all those peripheral collateral effects?

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:01.680
 Wouldn't a highly targeted drug be safer?

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:02.960
 It's a fantastic question.

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:05.120
 And it really highlights the vast difference

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:08.720
 between chronic disease management and acute trauma medicine.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:09.200
 Okay.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:15.760
 When a patient is in a systemic crisis, say anaphylactic shock or full cardiac arrest,

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:19.360
 their entire autonomic nervous system is collapsing.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:23.760
 The blood pressure has bottomed out because the vessels have gone flaccid.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.040
 The heart is fibrillating or has stopped entirely.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:28.800
 The airways might be swelling shut.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.320
 So everything is failing?

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:31.120
 Everything.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:35.200
 You do not have the luxury of time and you do not need a sniper rifle.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.280
 You absolutely need the grenade.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.440
 Because you need everything to reboot at once.

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:40.320
 Exactly.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:40.880
 Examine.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.920
 You need a sledgehammer that is going to violently jolt

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:46.000
 every survival mechanism simultaneously.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:51.040
 Epinephrine forces the heart to beat with massive force via beta one.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:54.880
 It forces the vessels to ruthlessly clamp down via alpha one

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.600
 to create enough blood pressure to keep the brain alive.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.240
 And it forces the lungs open via beta two.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.200
 It is the ultimate biological override switch.

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:03.440
 Okay.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:06.720
 That makes total sense in a literal life or death scenario.

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:08.880
 But what if you just want to raise the blood pressure

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.040
 without necessarily redlining the heart?

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:14.240
 That brings us to another critical drug in the notes.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:17.600
 Nor epinephrine, commonly known in the hospital as levafed.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:18.720
 Ah, levafed.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.200
 It's legendary and intensive care units.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:21.440
 Right.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.520
 The notes describe its profile as having intense alpha ettorinergic effects

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:31.360
 causing massive vasoconstriction and direct stimulating beta one effects on the heart.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:36.400
 But crucially, it states there is no stimulation to the beta two receptors in the lungs.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:42.000
 It's given by a continuous IV infusion to treat profound hypotension in a shock.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:46.000
 The clinical nickname for this drug, and it's a pretty grim one,

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:48.240
 used to be levafed, leave them dead.

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:48.720
 Oh, wow.

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:52.480
 Not because the drug itself kills people, but because historically,

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:56.240
 it was the absolute last resort for a patient who was circling the drain.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:58.560
 It is incredibly potent.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:18:59.440
 So it's powerful.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:00.480
 Very.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.320
 When a patient is in septic shock, their blood vessels dilate massively,

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:09.040
 and their blood pressure drops so low that blood can't even reach the kidneys or the brain.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:12.080
 Levafed acts almost like a chemical vise grip.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:13.440
 Squeezing the pipes again.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:14.000
 Yes.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.520
 It hits those alpha receptors and clamps the blood vessels so hard

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.640
 that it physically drags the blood pressure up out of the basement

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.920
 while giving the heart a moderate beta one boost to keep things moving.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:26.880
 It's fascinating how changing just the receptor affinity

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.440
 changes the entire clinical use case.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:34.080
 And then you have a drug like phenylephrine, brand name neosinephrine.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:39.920
 The source notes this works almost exclusively on the alpha adrenergic receptors.

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:41.840
 It's just pure squeeze.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.680
 Just pure squeeze, exactly.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:46.160
 It's used for short-term treatments to raise blood pressure

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:48.160
 without directly revving the heart's engine at all.

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:48.960
 Precisely.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:51.760
 If a patient's heart rate is already dangerously high

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.600
 but their blood pressure is critically low,

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.280
 you can't give them epinephrine

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:58.160
 because you'll push the heart rate even higher and cause a heart attack.

00:19:58.160 --> 00:19:59.120
 Oh, right.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:02.400
 You give phenylephrine to squeeze the pipes and raise the pressure

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:04.320
 while leaving the heart completely alone.

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:04.880
 Okay.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.440
 The final clinical application I want to pull from this section

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.960
 on adrenergic drugs has nothing to do with the heart or lungs.

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:12.400
 It's actually in the bladder.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:15.040
 The notes mentioned an indication for overactive bladder

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:18.880
 using a drug called Mira Beggron, brand name Mirbittrick.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:22.240
 It relaxes the detrucer muscle during the storage phase,

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:24.160
 increasing the bladder's capacity.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:27.440
 This is a beautiful example of harnessing the sympathetic response

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:28.800
 for a chronic condition.

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:30.960
 Think back to the bear in the woods again.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:31.520
 Right.

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.440
 When you're running your sympathetic nervous system

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:37.840
 tells the bladder smooth muscle, the detrucer, to deeply relax

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.480
 so it can stretch and hold urine without signaling in the brain

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:42.000
 that you need to stop and pee.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.640
 Because again, no bathroom breaks during a bear attack.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:45.200
 Exactly.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:48.720
 But patients with overactive bladder suffer from a detrucer muscle

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.840
 that is constantly spasming and contracting when it shouldn't.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.120
 By giving a highly targeted adrenergic drug

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:58.080
 that mimics that don't pee while running signal,

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.920
 you force the bladder to relax, giving the patient their quality of loft back.

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:02.960
 It's just brilliant.

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:03.280
 Okay.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:07.200
 So we've successfully mapped out how to floor the body's gas pedal.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:10.320
 We know how to speed it up, squeeze it tight, and open it wide.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.440
 But what happens when the engine is running too hot?

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:16.240
 What if the body's natural sympathetic response

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:18.160
 is the thing that is actually causing damage?

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.760
 Like if a patient's blood pressure is chronically high

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:23.760
 or their heart is working itself to death.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:24.800
 Exactly.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.760
 Then you need to figure out a way to cut the wires.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.120
 You need to block the signal.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.360
 Which brings us to the exact opposite side of the pharmacological coin.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:35.520
 Adigenic blocking drugs.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:38.880
 Because for every agonist that simulates a receptor lock,

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.520
 pharmaceutical science has developed an antagonist

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:43.760
 designed to jam a key in that lock and break it off.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.440
 So nothing else can get it.

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:46.160
 Right.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:50.000
 The source material defines these blockers as drugs that bind to

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:52.880
 adrogenic receptors, but instead of stimulating them,

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:56.880
 they inhibit or block the stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:00.560
 They're called "adrogenic antagonists" or "sympatholytics."

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:01.840
 Sympatholytic.

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:02.160
 Yeah.

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:05.680
 And that term is extremely descriptive if you break down the etymology.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:09.440
 The sephic's "lidic" means to break down, destroy, or halt.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:12.400
 Like a hemolytic disease destroys red blood cells.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:16.160
 A sympatholytic drug literally breaks the sympathetic response.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:18.240
 It stops the signal dead in its tracks.

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:22.240
 And just like the agonists, they're classified by the specific receptors they block.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:24.880
 Let's look at the alpha blockers first.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.160
 The mechanism here is totally logical.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:30.880
 If an alpha agonist causes the blood vessels to

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:34.960
 constrict in squeeze, an alpha blocker prevents that squeeze.

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:36.160
 Hey, relaxism.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:39.200
 The notes state they cause arterial and venous dilation,

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:44.000
 reducing peripheral vascular resistance and, consequently, lowering blood pressure.

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:47.680
 Imagine holding a garden hose and squeezing it tightly with your hand,

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:51.120
 the pressure of the water shooting out the end skyrockets, right?

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:51.680
 Right.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:55.280
 That is high blood pressure driven by sympathetic tone.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:59.600
 An alpha blocker chemically forces your hand to open up and drop the hose.

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:03.280
 The hose widens, the resistance disappears,

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:06.720
 and the pressure of the fluid inside immediately drops to a safe level.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.760
 So naturally, they're used to treat hypertension.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.640
 But the indications listed go far beyond just standard high blood pressure.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:17.440
 They're used to treat a condition called theochromocytoma,

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:21.840
 which is a rare tumor in the adrenal gland that constantly pumps out massive amounts of

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:27.200
 catecholamines, keeping their patients in this perpetual agonizing state of fight or flight.

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:28.400
 Which sounds awful.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:29.360
 It really does.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.360
 And they also mentioned treating Raynaud's disease,

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:38.000
 acrocyanosis, and frostbite.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:41.680
 In a patient with Raynaud's disease, the blood vessels in their extremities,

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.000
 like the fingers and the toes, are hypersensitive to cold or stress.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:47.120
 So they just clampshoot?

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:50.720
 They aggressively constrict, cutting off blood flow, turning the fingers

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.760
 stark white or blue and causing intense pain.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:58.160
 By administering an alpha blocker, you essentially sever the command wire,

00:23:58.160 --> 00:23:59.840
 telling those vessels to constrict.

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:04.560
 The vessels are forced to dilate, warm blood rushes back into the fingers,

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:05.840
 and the tissue is saved.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:11.360
 Speaking of saving tissue, the source of material highlights a very dramatic hospital

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.880
 emergency involving an alpha blocker called fentolamine.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:15.920
 Oh, this is a big one.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.920
 The node state that fentolamine quickly reverses the potent vasoconstrictive effects

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:26.400
 of extravacated vasopressors, such as norepinephrine,

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:29.520
 which restores blood flow and prevents tissue necrosis.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:35.840
 This is one of those incredibly high stress situations that nurses and ICU

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:37.360
 doctors train for constantly.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:42.720
 We just talked about how powerful a drug like levafed is at clamping down blood vessels.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:43.040
 Right.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:44.640
 The chemical vice grip?

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:49.040
 These drugs are caustic and potent and they are infused directly into a patient's vein

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:49.920
 through an IV line.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:55.600
 But what happens if the IV needle shifts and it accidentally pokes through the wall of the vein?

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:59.840
 The drug would just start pumping directly into the surrounding muscle and fat of the arm.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:00.800
 Exactly.

00:25:00.800 --> 00:25:06.160
 That is called extravization and it is an absolute nightmare scenario because suddenly

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:11.440
 this incredibly concentrated alpha agonist is bathing the local capillaries in the arm.

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:15.440
 The capillaries receive a massive localized signal to constrict.

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:15.920
 Oh, no.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:20.560
 They clamp down so violently that they act like a chemical tourniquet.

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:23.840
 All blood flow in that area of the arm completely stops.

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:27.840
 The tissue is instantly starved of oxygen and within minutes it begins to

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:31.120
 literally suffocate and die. That is tissue necrosis.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:32.880
 The arm will physically turn black.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:35.600
 Wait till the rescue drug that's supposed to be saving their life

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:38.720
 is literally strangling the patient's arm from the inside out.

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:38.960
 Yes.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:43.280
 And the immediate protocol to save the limb is to grab a syringe of phyntolamine,

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:49.360
 the pure alpha blocker and inject it in a circle directly into the dying tissue around the IV site.

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:50.720
 Just right into the tissue.

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:51.520
 Right into it.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:56.560
 The phyntolamine rushes in, aggressively kicks the levafed off the alpha receptors and

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.440
 forces the capillaries back open. Blood rushes back in and you literally watch

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:03.280
 the tissue turn pink again.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:06.240
 It is a spectacular pharmacological rescue.

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:07.760
 That is just wild.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:09.200
 The sheer precision of it.

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.480
 Okay. Now moving from the arm to urology,

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.920
 there is a very common use for alpha blockers regarding the prostate.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:20.080
 The most discussed how blocking alpha receptors on the prostate gland and bladder

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:26.400
 decreases resistance to urinary outflow, which treats BPH or benign prostatic hyperplasia.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:30.160
 The drug they highlight here is Tamsalocin, brand name Flowmax.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:35.120
 To understand why this works, you have to picture the anatomy of the male urinary tract.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.680
 The prostate gland is basically shaped like a donut.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:41.200
 And the urethra, the tube that carries urine out of the bladder,

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:43.840
 runs right through the center of the donut hole.

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:44.880
 Okay. I can picture that.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:48.960
 As men age, the prostate often slowly enlarges.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:53.440
 The donut gets thicker and it begins to physically pinch the urethra shut,

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:56.080
 making it incredibly difficult to urinate.

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:58.640
 But the prostate isn't just a static lump of tissue, right?

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.240
 It has smooth muscle in it.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:03.760
 Exactly. It is packed with alpha-1 receptors.

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:08.400
 By giving a patient Tamsalocin, the drug targets those specific receptors in the prostate

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:11.440
 and tells the smooth muscle to deeply relax.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:15.120
 The donut softens, the tension on the urethra is released,

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:17.920
 the channel opens up and urine can flow normally again.

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.520
 The nose also mentioned an interesting off-label use for this.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:28.320
 While Tamsalocin is primarily for male BPH, it is also frequently used in female patients

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:29.920
 who have kidney stones.

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:33.040
 The structural logic holds perfectly true here.

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:36.880
 The ureters, the tubes that carry urine from the kidneys down to the bladder,

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:38.800
 are lined with smooth muscle.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:41.600
 If a jagged kidney stone gets stuck in the tube,

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:44.800
 the muscle naturally spasms and clamps down around it,

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:48.800
 which is why kidney stones cause some of the most excruciating pain known to medicine.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:53.120
 Giving Tamsalocin relaxes the smooth muscle of the ureter,

00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:56.160
 widening the tube just enough for the stone to slip past.

00:27:56.160 --> 00:28:00.000
 The material does flag a very serious contraindication for Tamsalocin, though.

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:05.680
 Concurrent use of erectile dysfunction drugs, such as sildenafil or Viagra.

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:08.320
 Which makes perfect hemodynamic sense.

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:12.480
 Alpha blockers like Tamsalocin cause systemic vasodilation.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.360
 They relax smooth muscle and widen blood vessels.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:15.920
 Right.

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:20.080
 Drugs like sildenafil also cause profound systemic vasodilation

00:28:20.080 --> 00:28:22.000
 through a totally different pathway.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:25.120
 If a patient takes both at the exact same time,

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:28.880
 it is a recipe for a catastrophic drop in blood pressure.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:30.640
 The pipes open way too wide.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:32.560
 There isn't enough fluid to maintain pressure.

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:35.360
 The brain is deprived of oxygen and the patient will pass out

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:37.120
 or go into severe hypotensive shock.

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:38.960
 That makes a long sense.

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:42.960
 Okay, let's pivot back to the heart and talk about beta blockers.

