 Mitoprolol, atenolol, nebivolol, soprolol, asabutylol, vataxylol, and esmalol.

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:50.960
 It is a bit ridiculous.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.440
 I know, but it is one of those sticky memory tricks

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:56.000
 that every nursing and medical student uses.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:00.800
 When you're staring at a multiple choice board exam with a list of 10 LOL drugs,

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:03.600
 and you need to find the one that is safe for an asthmatic patient

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:07.600
 because it only targets the heart, you immediately look for the man babe drugs.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.920
 And what do those cardio-selective drugs actually do when they hit the heart?

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:16.240
 The note state they reduced SNS stimulation, decreased heart rate,

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:20.080
 prolonged essay node recovery, slow conduction through the AV node,

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:23.040
 and decreased myocardial contractility.

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:26.880
 Basically, they reverse every single thing we talked about in the gas pedal section.

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:29.440
 They lower the myocardial oxygen demand.

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:31.440
 So they force the heart to chill out.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.120
 Think of the heart as a car engine.

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:35.920
 If you constantly run an engine at 8,000 RPMs,

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.960
 it's going to run incredibly hot, burn a massive amount of fuel,

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:40.560
 and eventually wear out and break.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:41.200
 Yeah.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:42.880
 By putting a beta-one blocker on board,

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:45.920
 you are installing a chemical governor on the engine.

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:49.200
 You are physically preventing the adrenaline from redlining the heart.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:54.000
 The heart beats slower, it beats with slightly less violent force,

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.320
 and because it is working less, it demands significantly less oxygen from the blood.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.920
 Which explains the massive list of indications for these drugs.

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:06.000
 They're used for angina, which is chest pain caused by the heart,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.720
 literally starving for oxygen because it's working too hard.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:12.560
 They're used as class two anti-disarithmics,

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:16.560
 to force a chaotic electrical system to slow down and find a steady rhythm.

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:18.720
 They're used for heart failure.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:23.600
 But the most prominent use highlighted is that they are cardio-protective.

00:31:23.600 --> 00:31:25.600
 Cardio-protective, yes.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:29.760
 The notes say they inhibit stimulation from circulating catecholamines,

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:31.840
 which is why they are heavily used in patients

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.960
 who have recently suffered a myocardial infarction or a heart attack.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:38.560
 This is a beautiful piece of physiological shielding.

00:31:38.560 --> 00:31:39.920
 When a patient has a heart attack,

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:42.720
 a portion of their heart muscle actually dies due to a blockage.

00:31:43.360 --> 00:31:46.880
 The brain senses this massive trauma and, well, it panics.

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:50.160
 It dumps immense amounts of adrenaline into the blood

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:52.320
 to try and force the failing heart to pump harder.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:55.760
 But that is the absolute worst thing that could happen.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:56.800
 Why is that bad?

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:57.680
 Don't you want it to pump?

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:03.040
 Whipping a damaged dime heart to work harder will only expand the zone of dead tissue

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:04.640
 and cause lethal arrhythmias.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.760
 By immediately giving the patient a beta blocker,

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:10.080
 you build a chemical shield around the heart.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:12.240
 The adrenaline washes over the heart,

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:13.920
 but can't get into the receptors.

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:16.480
 The heart is forced to rest and heal.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:17.440
 That is incredible.

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:18.560
 A chemical shield.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:19.920
 Now, on the other side of the aisle,

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:22.400
 we have the non-selective beta blockers.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:26.880
 Examples given are carvatalol, labatalol, and propanolol.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:28.560
 Propranolol is a huge one.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:30.240
 The material notes that these drugs do

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:32.320
 all the cardio-protective things we just discussed.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:34.320
 But because they are non-selective,

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:38.240
 they also drift over to the lungs and block the beta-2 receptors.

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:41.440
 And blocking those receptors constricts the bronchioles,

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:44.160
 causing narrowing of airways and shortness of breath.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:46.480
 Right, because you are pulling away the sympathetic tone

00:32:46.480 --> 00:32:49.360
 that normally keeps those airways propped open wide.

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:51.760
 And this brings up a hugely logistical question for me.

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:55.120
 Wait, if we know that non-selective beta blockers

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:57.440
 constrict the airways and can cause breathing issues.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:58.240
 Yes.

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:00.640
 And we just established that selective beta-1 drugs,

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:05.840
 the man-babe drugs, exist, and they perfectly spare the lungs.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:09.840
 Why would anyone ever prescribe a non-selective beta blocker?

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:13.440
 Why risk a patient's lungs by using something like propanolol

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:15.200
 if you have a perfectly targeted alternative?

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:16.880
 It's an excellent question.

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.120
 And it speaks to the complex reality

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:21.280
 of treating systemic human beings,

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:23.040
 rather than just isolated organs.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.880
 Yes, blocking beta-2 causes some airway construction

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:30.160
 for a patient with asthma or severe COPD,

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:32.640
 giving them propanolol could trigger a fatal asthma attack.

